; =====================================================================================
; PYTHON PROJECT SETUP STEPS
; =====================================================================================
;
; HOW TO USE THIS FILE:
; 1. Each [ProjectType] section defines a different project setup or learning path
; 2. Steps are pipe-delimited: step|command|directions|input_needed|explanation
; 3. Lines starting with ; are comments (ignored)
; 4. Empty lines are ignored
;
; ENVIRONMENT MANAGEMENT OPTIONS:
; - venv: Built into Python, simple, works everywhere
; - Anaconda/Miniconda: Best for data science, handles complex deps
; - Poetry: Modern packaging, great for publishing packages
; - Pipenv: Combines pip + virtualenv, auto-generates lockfile
;
; =====================================================================================

[Python venv (Standard - Recommended for Beginners)]
; Built-in Python virtual environment - no extra tools needed
; This is the simplest approach and comes with Python itself
1|mkdir MyPythonProject && cd MyPythonProject|Create project folder|Project name|Creates your project directory. Use a descriptive name like my-web-scraper or data-analysis.
2|python -m venv venv|Create virtual environment|None|Creates isolated Python environment in ./venv folder. This keeps project dependencies separate from system Python.
3|venv\Scripts\activate|Activate environment (Windows)|None|Activates venv. Your prompt shows (venv) prefix. IMPORTANT: Run this every time you work on the project!
4|source venv/bin/activate|Activate environment (Mac/Linux)|None|Alternative activation for Unix systems. Use this instead of step 3 on Mac or Linux.
5|pip install --upgrade pip|Upgrade pip|None|Ensures you have the latest pip version. Older pip versions may have bugs or security issues.
6|pip install <package_name>|Install packages|Package names|Install packages like: pip install requests flask pandas. Each package gets isolated to this venv.
7|pip freeze > requirements.txt|Save dependencies|None|Creates requirements.txt listing all installed packages with versions. Others can recreate your environment.
8|pip install -r requirements.txt|Install from requirements|None|Run this to install all packages from requirements.txt (useful when cloning a project).
9|deactivate|Exit virtual environment|None|Returns to system Python. Your (venv) prefix disappears from the prompt.

[Anaconda/Miniconda (Best for Data Science)]
; Data science focused - great for numpy, pandas, jupyter, ML libraries
; Conda handles complex C dependencies that pip sometimes struggles with
1|conda create -n myenv python=3.11|Create conda environment|Env name, Python version|Creates isolated conda environment. Replace myenv with your project name. Python version is optional.
2|conda activate myenv|Activate environment|None|Activates the conda environment. Prompt shows (myenv). Use this instead of source activate on newer conda.
3|conda install numpy pandas matplotlib|Install data science packages|Package names|Conda handles complex dependencies like BLAS, MKL for numpy better than pip.
4|conda install jupyter|Install Jupyter|None|Adds Jupyter notebooks support for interactive data exploration and visualization.
5|jupyter notebook|Start Jupyter|None|Opens Jupyter in your browser at http://localhost:8888. Create .ipynb notebooks for analysis.
6|conda install scikit-learn|Install ML packages|None|scikit-learn for machine learning. Also try: conda install tensorflow pytorch (check channels).
7|conda env export > environment.yml|Export environment|None|Creates portable environment definition. Others can recreate with: conda env create -f environment.yml
8|conda deactivate|Exit environment|None|Returns to base conda environment.

[Poetry (Modern Dependency Management)]
; Modern Python packaging and dependency management
; Great for publishing packages to PyPI, strict version resolution
1|pip install poetry|Install Poetry (one-time)|None|One-time global installation of Poetry. Only need to do this once per system.
2|poetry new my-project|Create new project|Project name|Creates project with proper structure: src folder, pyproject.toml, tests folder, README.
3|cd my-project|Enter project directory|None|Navigate into the created project folder.
4|poetry add requests|Add a dependency|Package name|Adds package to pyproject.toml and installs it. Automatically resolves compatible versions.
5|poetry add pytest --group dev|Add dev dependency|Package name|Adds development-only dependencies (testing, linting) separate from production deps.
6|poetry install|Install all dependencies|None|Installs everything from pyproject.toml. Creates poetry.lock for reproducible builds.
7|poetry shell|Activate environment|None|Enters the Poetry-managed virtual environment. Alternative to activating venv manually.
8|poetry run python main.py|Run without activating|Script name|Runs Python in the environment without activating shell. Great for scripts and automation.
9|poetry build|Build package|None|Creates distributable wheel/sdist files in dist/ folder for publishing.

[Pipenv (Pip + Virtualenv Combined)]
; Combines pip and virtualenv into one tool
; Auto-generates Pipfile and Pipfile.lock for reproducible installs
1|pip install pipenv|Install Pipenv (one-time)|None|One-time global installation of Pipenv.
2|mkdir myproject && cd myproject|Create project folder|Project name|Creates your project directory.
3|pipenv install|Create environment|None|Creates Pipfile and virtual environment. If Pipfile exists, installs from it.
4|pipenv install requests flask|Install packages|Package names|Adds packages to Pipfile automatically and installs them.
5|pipenv install pytest --dev|Install dev packages|Package name|Separates development dependencies from production in Pipfile.
6|pipenv shell|Activate environment|None|Enters the Pipenv shell (virtual environment).
7|pipenv run python app.py|Run without shell|Script name|Runs command in environment without activating shell.
8|pipenv lock|Generate lockfile|None|Creates Pipfile.lock for reproducible installs. Auto-generated on install but can be manually updated.
9|pipenv sync|Install from lockfile|None|Installs exactly what's in Pipfile.lock (useful for deployment).

; =====================================================================================
; PYTHON FUNDAMENTALS - SYNTAX LEARNING PATH
; =====================================================================================
; These steps are for learning Python syntax, not project setup
; Use them as a reference or teaching guide
; =====================================================================================

[Python Basics - Variables & Types]
; Fundamental building blocks of Python
1|x = 5|Integer variable|Variable name|Python uses dynamic typing - no need to declare type. x is now an integer.
2|name = "hello"|String variable|String value|Strings can use single or double quotes. 'hello' and "hello" are identical.
3|price = 19.99|Float variable|Decimal number|Floating point numbers for decimals. Python handles precision automatically.
4|is_valid = True|Boolean variable|True/False|Booleans are capitalized: True, False (not true/false like JavaScript).
5|print(type(x))|Check variable type|Any variable|type() returns the class of a variable. Useful for debugging.
6|str(5) + " items"|Type conversion|Value to convert|Convert between types: str(), int(), float(), bool(), list(), etc.

[Python Basics - Collections]
; Lists, dictionaries, tuples, sets
1|my_list = [1, 2, 3]|Create a list|List elements|Ordered, mutable (changeable) sequence. Use [] syntax. Can contain mixed types.
2|my_list.append(4)|Add to list|Value to add|Adds element to end. Other methods: insert(), extend(), remove(), pop().
3|my_list[0]|Access by index|Index number|Zero-indexed! First element is [0], last is [-1]. Slicing: my_list[1:3]
4|my_dict = {"key": "value"}|Create a dictionary|Key-value pairs|Key-value pairs for fast lookups. Keys must be immutable (strings, numbers, tuples).
5|my_dict["new_key"] = "new_value"|Add to dictionary|Key and value|Dictionaries are mutable. Access with my_dict["key"] or my_dict.get("key").
6|my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)|Create a tuple|Tuple elements|Immutable sequence - cannot be changed after creation. Good for fixed data.
7|my_set = {1, 2, 3}|Create a set|Set elements|Unordered, unique elements only. Great for removing duplicates: set(my_list).

[Python Basics - Control Flow]
; if/else, loops, comprehensions
1|if x > 5:|If statement|Condition|Colon starts a block. Next lines must be indented. No braces needed!
2|elif x == 5:|Else if|Condition|Python uses elif, not else if. Can chain multiple elif statements.
3|else:|Else clause|None|Catches all cases not matched by if/elif. Optional.
4|for item in items:|For loop|Iterable|Iterates directly over items, not indices. Much cleaner than for(i=0;i<len;i++).
5|for i in range(10):|Range-based loop|Range params|range(10) = 0-9, range(1,10) = 1-9, range(0,10,2) = 0,2,4,6,8
6|while condition:|While loop|Condition|Loops while condition is True. Don't forget to update condition to avoid infinite loop!
7|[x*2 for x in items]|List comprehension|Expression|Concise way to create lists. Equivalent to for loop but more Pythonic.

[Python Basics - Functions]
; Defining and using functions
1|def greet(name):|Define function|Function name, params|Use def keyword. Colon starts function body. Indentation defines scope.
2|return f"Hello {name}"|Return value|Value to return|f-strings (f"...") allow embedded expressions. return exits function with value.
3|def add(a, b=0):|Default parameters|Param name, default|Parameters with defaults are optional. Must come after required params.
4|greet("World")|Call function|Arguments|Pass arguments by position or name: greet(name="World")
5|*args|Variable positional args|None|Collects extra positional args as tuple: def func(*args)
6|**kwargs|Variable keyword args|None|Collects extra keyword args as dict: def func(**kwargs)
7|lambda x: x * 2|Lambda function|Expression|Anonymous single-expression function. Good for map(), filter(), sort key.

[Python Basics - Classes & OOP]
; Object-oriented programming fundamentals
1|class Dog:|Define class|Class name|Use class keyword. Convention: CamelCase names for classes.
2|def __init__(self, name):|Constructor|Parameters|__init__ is called when creating instance. self refers to the instance.
3|self.name = name|Instance attribute|Attribute name|Attributes defined in __init__ belong to each instance.
4|def bark(self):|Instance method|Method name|Methods take self as first param. Access instance attrs with self.attribute.
5|my_dog = Dog("Rex")|Create instance|Constructor args|Creates new Dog object. __init__ is automatically called.
6|my_dog.bark()|Call method|None|Access methods with dot notation. self is passed automatically.
7|class Poodle(Dog):|Inheritance|Parent class|Poodle inherits from Dog. Gets all attributes and methods of Dog.

[Python Basics - File I/O]
; Reading and writing files
1|with open("file.txt", "r") as f:|Open file for reading|Filename|with statement auto-closes file. "r" = read mode.
2|content = f.read()|Read entire file|None|Returns entire file as string. For large files, consider f.readline() or iteration.
3|lines = f.readlines()|Read all lines|None|Returns list of lines. Each line includes \n newline character.
4|for line in f:|Iterate over lines|None|Memory-efficient way to process large files line by line.
5|with open("file.txt", "w") as f:|Open for writing|Filename|"w" = write (overwrites). "a" = append. "x" = create (fails if exists).
6|f.write("Hello\n")|Write to file|Content|Writes string to file. Remember to add \n for newlines.
7|import json|Import JSON module|None|Built-in module for JSON. json.load(f), json.dump(data, f), json.loads(str), json.dumps(obj).

[Python Basics - Error Handling]
; try/except and exception handling
1|try:|Try block|None|Code that might raise an exception goes here.
2|except ValueError:|Catch specific error|Exception type|Catches only ValueError. Can catch multiple: except (ValueError, TypeError):
3|except Exception as e:|Catch any exception|Variable name|Exception stores the error object. Access message with str(e).
4|else:|Success block|None|Runs only if no exception was raised. Optional.
5|finally:|Always runs|None|Runs whether or not exception occurred. Good for cleanup (closing files).
6|raise ValueError("msg")|Raise exception|Exception type, message|Manually raise an exception. Use for input validation, error conditions.

